  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/6/6b/Tales_of_Suspense_Vol_1_55.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "We pick up right where last issue left off, with Iron Man, drained of most of his power, about to get destroyed by the Mandarin, but then the text reads that \"a <i>smile</i> appears on the iron countenance of the trapped man.\"&nbsp; Furthermore, Mandarin actually says \"you dare smile in the face of death?\"&nbsp; (Reviewer's Note:&nbsp; there is no hint of a smile on the iron mask, a fact which I'm betting was pointed out by countless readers!)\n\nHe bluffs Mandarin into believing that Stark is still out there, destroying his anti-missile weapon (which was the reason Iron Man came to the Orient in the first place, last issue).&nbsp; While the Mandarin investigates this claim, it provides time for Iron Man's armor to recharge with its \"miniature generator,\" and he finally is able to break free.\n\nHe quietly follows Mandarin to the room where he's stored all the intercepted missiles.&nbsp; He watches as he aims an \"interceptor ray\" at another and flies off to rescue it, which he does, but gets pulled down himself by the ray, back to Mandarin's castle, but he smashes everything, leading to an explosion.&nbsp; Mandarin is <i>beyond</i> pissed and vows to destroy Iron Man!\n\nWhat follows is a handful of pages of cat and mouse, with Mandarin seeming to toy with his enemy with an endless parade of ring gadgets, plucked from the imagination of either Stan Lee or Don Heck (or both).&nbsp; Mandarin fires a disintegrator beam, becomes giant-sized and then duplicates himself, but Iron Man evades and makes his way back toward where the missiles are stored and sends them home before completely destroying the interceptor ray.&nbsp; Mandarin is left swearing that he'll \"never stop searching for him!\"\n\nStark then returns to the base in Viet Nam and catches a ride home.&nbsp; Happy and Pepper are thrilled to see him.",
  "grade_one_liner": "Write something",
  "subtitle": "Write something",
  "review_text": "while it was good to see Iron Man in full action this issue, the problem remains with the Mandarin's rings, which continue to provide the cheap writer's crutch of inventing anything you feel like in the moment to threaten Iron Man.&nbsp; The battle action was largely fine, if you happen to like action, but there was never much body-to-body contact here - Mandarin relied solely on his rings to fight Iron Man, who took advantage and fled to the control room to recover the missiles, destroy the panel and flee, leaving Mandarin only with ruins and needing to pick up the pieces of his plan and start over, falling just short of uttering the \"I'll get you for this, Iron Man!\" trope, which thankfully was <i>not</i> present, but it was <i>darn</i> close!\n\nThe side plot of Happy having to fill in for Stark made no sense though - he's a former boxer who now drives for Stark - what on Earth was he doing filling in?&nbsp; This was utter nonsense, and as such, dropped the final grade.&nbsp; Happy and Pepper continue to feel more force-fed on the reader, and in this case, it was a bad move that didn't need to be there.&nbsp; Their presence in the stories is nothing like the characters that surround Peter Parker, which mostly fit nicely <i>into</i>, or actually contribute <i>to</i> the stories.\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nThe story was more than readable, but never impressed, though the back-up feature on Iron Man's armor and Potts pin-up page were nice additions that boosted the grade to what would have been a VG/FN rating.&nbsp; (But <i>wait!</i>  See <i>Tales of the Watcher</i> below for why this book got a full FN rating instead!)\n\nI do feel that a call-out needs to go to that awesome cover!&nbsp; One of the more memorable ones, in my opinion.\n\nA second, short Iron Man feature shows how Tony changes to Iron Man and close-ups of many of the various gadgets he has.&nbsp; There is also a Pepper Potts pin-up page as well.\n\nFinally, it's worth noting that this issue gets an entire point boost because of its wonderful back-up tale called \"The Sun-Stealers!\"&nbsp; Go down to the Side Note and read that description, or better yet, if you have a copy of the original issue, it's a <b>must-read!</b>",
  "side_note": [
    "<b>Tales of the Watcher:&nbsp; </b>\"The Sun-Stealer\"\n[BR] (by Stan Lee and Larry Lieber)\n\nThe Watcher observes Earth from the Moon, one of his favorite places, when suddenly, a craft lands and requests aid.&nbsp; When he explains he can only observe, the occupant is triumphant, for he has come from another galaxy.&nbsp; His race's sun is growing weaker and burning out.&nbsp; Their scientists have built a machine that can steal a sun from elsewhere -- one that must be the same size and radiance as theirs, and they finally find Sol.&nbsp; They'll be able to drag it to their world \"at a hundred times the speed of light\" and be saved.&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Gee, even at <i>that</i> speed, how long would it take to drag a sun from one galaxy to another?&nbsp; I guess the reader should not ask these questions!)]\n\nThe Watcher presses the occupant for full details on the plan, seeing as he cannot interfere.&nbsp; Knowing this, the intruder gladly obliges!&nbsp; He goes on and on about his master plan to steal the sun, with the Watcher keeping him occupied, peppering him with one question after another about his evil plot, until he finally turns to find that the place where he parked his spacecraft was a bog!&nbsp; The ship has sunk into it and is lost!\n\nCurse the Watcher!&nbsp; He <i>had </i>to have known about this!&nbsp; He can't even contact his people!&nbsp; He's trapped here!\n\nSoon the creature's air will run out, so he pleads for the Watcher's help, only to be told, \"I <i>cannot</i> help you!&nbsp; I am forbidden to act! ... I can do nothing but stand...and watch!\"&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=italic; text=Hahahaha!]\n\nThis is nothing but a complete rip-off of the ending to my favorite back-up story from last year, the fantastic [LINK:1963:TTA_47_ant0|TTA #47]'s \"Target...Earth!\"&nbsp; But I enjoyed that story so much that I didn't care, and as such, retroactively boosted my final rating for this issue by an entire point, making it full mid-grade in the end, despite the fact that the story is ridiculous and hopelessly far-fetched.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; text=The addition of the Watcher being \"not\" involved was a wonderful twist.]\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=But... I <i>do</i> wonder if he's allowed to at least bury the body after the intruder's air runs out...]\n\nThe Watcher is described on the splash pages as the \"most powerful being in the galaxy.\"&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=(Who knew?)]"
  ],
  "ownership": "I was lucky enough to get a VG copy as part of the 2000 collection, for which I paid nearly $8, ultimately selling it for close to $40 in December 2020, yielding me a net of around $28, which I deemed satisfactory.",
  "highlight": "Write something"
}